.Dd October 29, 2026
.Dt LOCALEDEF 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm localedef
.Nd make a locale from its definition
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl c
.Op Fl v
.Op Fl U
.Op Fl f Ar charmap
.Op Fl w Ar widthfile
.Op Fl i Ar sourcefile
.Op Fl u Ar codeset
.Ar localename
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads a locale's definition, in the POSIX
.Xr locale 5
source format (from
.Ar sourcefile ,
or the standard input), and writes the files the C library reads for
it into the directory
.Ar localename :
.Pa LC_CTYPE ,
.Pa LC_COLLATE ,
.Pa LC_MONETARY ,
.Pa LC_NUMERIC ,
.Pa LC_TIME
and
.Pa LC_MESSAGES/LC_MESSAGES ,
one for each category the definition has.
Installed in
.Pa /usr/share/locale ,
the locale can then be named in
.Ev LANG
or the
.Ev LC_*
variables.
.Pp
Characters are written by their symbolic names, as
.Ql <U00E9> ,
which
.Ar charmap
gives the bytes of.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl c
Write the files even when there were warnings.
.It Fl f Ar charmap
The character set description.
.It Fl i Ar sourcefile
Read the definition from
.Ar sourcefile .
.It Fl U
Ignore characters the charmap does not define.
.It Fl u Ar codeset
The codeset, when there is no charmap.
.It Fl v
Say what is written.
.It Fl w Ar widthfile
The display widths of the characters.
.El
.Pp
The system collates by code point: the order an
.Ql LC_COLLATE
definition gives is not kept (
.Nm
says so), and the
.Pa LC_COLLATE
written sorts by code point, as every locale of the system does.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 when there were warnings (and no
.Fl c ) ,
4 or more on an error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Dl localedef -f UTF-8.cm -i es_PE.src /usr/share/locale/es_PE.UTF-8
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr locale 1 ,
.Xr setlocale 3 ,
.Xr locale 5
.Sh HISTORY
Minkar's
.Nm
is FreeBSD's, which Garrett D'Amore wrote for illumos at Nexenta; this
manual is Minkar's.
